story.append(Spacer(1,40));p('SORTLOOP / DEEP LEARNING PROJECT');story.append(Paragraph('A second life starts<br/>with a first look.',styles['TitleSL']));p('Six-class waste image classification using a convolutional neural network');story.append(Spacer(1,28));table([['Measured outcome','Held-out test'],['Accuracy',f"{M['accuracy']:.2%} (320 / 379 correct)"],['Macro F1',f"{M['report']['macro avg']['f1-score']:.2%}"],['Data','2,524 images; six material classes'],['Model','MobileNetV3-Small CNN with transfer learning'],['Evaluation','Stratified train/validation/test split']],[150,345]);p('Prepared 11 November 2026. Course context: Deep Learning, International Program in Informatics, Universitas Islam Indonesia, odd semester 2026/2027.');p('This report follows the five sections in the exam brief. All reported experimental values were produced by the included implementation.');p('Author: Abdullah Al-Hamidi | Student ID: 24523229');p('GitHub repository: https://github.com/alhamidi1/sortloop');
page('1. Project & case study')
heading('Problem and objective');p('This project studies supervised image classification for six waste categories: cardboard, glass, metal, paper, plastic and trash. Its objective is to train and evaluate a convolutional neural network that predicts the dataset label for one image.');p('Success means a reproducible training procedure, performance above a simple majority-class baseline on a held-out test split, and a careful analysis of class-level strengths and errors.');heading('Why CNN instead of YOLO');p('TrashNet provides one image-level label per photograph, not bounding boxes. A CNN classifier directly matches those labels. YOLO would be appropriate for locating multiple objects in a scene, but would require representative multi-object images and box annotations. CNN classification is therefore the better match to this dataset and project objective.');heading('Scope');p('The experiment predicts one of six known labels for each image. It does not determine local recyclability rules, identify hazardous materials, or establish performance in a real collection facility.');
page('2. Data & preprocessing')
p('Source: TrashNet by Gary Thung and Mindy Yang (2016), downloaded from the public GitHub repository. Original resized archive: 2,527 images, largely photographed on white posterboard under sunlight or room lighting. Original class counts: cardboard 403, glass 501, metal 410, paper 594, plastic 482, trash 137. The upstream dataset license and attribution are retained in the project files.');p('SHA-256 hashes identify and remove three exact duplicate files before splitting. The resulting 2,524 images are stratified into 1,766 training, 379 validation and 379 test examples, using seed 42. The explicit file lists are saved in ml/splits.json. Augmentations are applied after splitting and only to training examples.');
splits=json.load(open(R/'ml/splits.json'));rows=[['Material','Train','Validation','Test']]
for c in M['classes']:rows.append([c,*[sum(x.startswith(c+'/') for x in splits[k]) for k in ['train','validation','test']]])
table(rows,[150,100,100,100]);heading('Input pipeline');p('Decode to RGB; resize to 160 x 160; convert pixel values from 0-255 to 0-1; normalize per channel using means (0.485, 0.456, 0.406) and standard deviations (0.229, 0.224, 0.225). These match ImageNet pretraining statistics. Direct resizing changes aspect ratio but provides a consistent model input shape.');p('For each training image, cache features from one unaugmented view and two independently randomized views. Augmentation first resizes to 180 x 180, samples a RandomResizedCrop to 160 with scale 0.75-1.0, applies a random horizontal flip, then ColorJitter with brightness/contrast 0.15, saturation 0.10 and hue 0.02. This tests moderate pose and lighting variation without changing material labels. Cached augmentations are fixed for all 80 head-training epochs.');p('Limitations: exact deduplication does not remove near duplicates or identify multiple photos of the same physical object. The random image split may be optimistic. A future evaluation should group images by object and capture session.');
page('3. Model implementation')
p('Transfer learning uses Torchvision MobileNetV3-Small with IMAGENET1K_V1 weights. The original ImageNet classifier is discarded. The frozen feature stack, adaptive global average pooling and flatten produce 576 features. A new linear layer maps 576 features to six logits. Softmax is applied only for display/inference; cross-entropy consumes raw logits.');table([['Component','Implementation','Purpose'],['Stem','3x3 Conv, stride 2, 3 to 16 channels; BatchNorm; Hardswish','Early spatial feature extraction'],['11 inverted residual blocks','1x1 expansion; 3x3 or 5x5 depthwise convolution; squeeze-excitation where configured; 1x1 projection','Efficient hierarchical visual features'],['Final feature convolution','1x1 Conv, 96 to 576; BatchNorm; Hardswish','Build compact feature representation'],['Pooling + flatten','Adaptive average pooling to 1x1; flatten','576-dimensional vector'],['Trained head','Linear(576, 6), with bias','Learn the six material categories']],[100,220,175]);p('The exact model contains 52 Conv2d modules (including squeeze-excitation convolutions), one adaptive global pooling stage in the main path and one final Linear classifier. BatchNorm, ReLU, Hardswish and Hardsigmoid activations follow the Torchvision implementation. Squeeze-excitation blocks additionally use their own internal pooling. The complete instantiated architecture is included as ml/architecture.txt.');p(f"Total parameters: {M['parameters']:,}. Frozen CNN parameters: {M['parameters']-M['trainable_parameters']:,}. Trained head parameters: {M['trainable_parameters']:,} = 576 x 6 + 6. Freezing the extractor reduces compute and overfitting risk on this small dataset, at the cost of limited domain adaptation.");p('Implementation is in ml/train.py, reproduced in Appendix A. The exported ONNX model provides a portable copy of the trained network; the PyTorch model and export-parity record are included with the source.');
page('4. Training & evaluation')
table([['Setting','Value'],['Optimizer','AdamW; learning rate 0.003; weight decay 0.02'],['Loss','Class-weighted cross-entropy for training; unweighted validation cross-entropy'],['Class weighting','N_train / (6 x count_class)'],['Batch size / epochs','128 feature vectors / 80 epochs'],['Checkpoint selection','Minimum validation loss; selected epoch 9'],['Randomness','Python, NumPy and Torch seeds = 42'],['Compute','CPU, 3 Torch threads; measured training/extraction time 50 seconds'],['Evaluation','One final held-out test evaluation after checkpoint selection']],[150,345]);story.append(Image(str(D/'training.png'),width=495,height=176));p('The dashed line marks the selected checkpoint. Later validation accuracy occasionally rises, but validation loss is the preselected selection criterion. Weighted training loss and unweighted validation loss differ in weighting and data augmentation, so their absolute levels should not be compared as identical objectives.');heading('Metrics');p('Accuracy = correct / total. For each class, precision = TP / (TP + FP), recall = TP / (TP + FN), and F1 = 2PR / (P + R). Macro F1 averages the six class F1 scores equally, preventing the large paper class from dominating the summary.');rows=[['Class','Precision','Recall','F1','Test n']]
for c in M['classes']:
 r=M['report'][c];rows.append([c,f"{r['precision']:.3f}",f"{r['recall']:.3f}",f"{r['f1-score']:.3f}",int(r['support'])])
rows.append(['Macro average',f"{M['report']['macro avg']['precision']:.3f}",f"{M['report']['macro avg']['recall']:.3f}",f"{M['report']['macro avg']['f1-score']:.3f}",379]);table(rows,[115,95,95,95,95]);
page('4. Evaluation, continued')
story.append(Image(str(D/'confusion.png'),width=470,height=336));p('The classifier correctly predicts 320 of 379 test images (84.43%). A majority-class baseline that always predicts paper would achieve 89/379 = 23.48% accuracy on this test set. The CNN clearly outperforms that simple baseline, although stronger trained baselines were not tested.');p('A Wilson 95% binomial confidence interval for test accuracy is approximately 80.4%-87.7%. This interval describes finite-sample uncertainty conditional on the split; it does not account for domain shift, repeated objects or model-selection uncertainty.');p('Cardboard is strongest (F1 0.926). Trash is weakest (F1 0.682), with only 21 test examples and a broad, heterogeneous class. Eight glass examples are mistaken for plastic and six for metal. Transparent surfaces, reflections and similar shapes are plausible explanations, but systematic image-level error analysis is required to establish causes.');p('The ONNX export matches PyTorch with maximum logit error below 0.0001. This numerical parity check compares the exported model with PyTorch on identical input tensors.');
page('5. Analysis & conclusion')
heading('What the results support');p('The project meets its technical objective on this dataset split: a supervised CNN was trained on relevant image labels and evaluated on an unseen test set. Its 84.43% accuracy and 82.61% macro F1 are substantially above the 23.48% majority-class baseline.');heading('Limitations');p('The dataset is small and consists largely of isolated items on plain backgrounds. The random image split may be optimistic because near-duplicate or repeated-object images may remain across subsets. These results do not establish performance in a real sorting facility. The classifier is closed-set and always predicts one of the six known labels.');p('The heterogeneous trash category is the weakest class (F1 0.682) and has only 21 test examples. Its broad label and low sample count limit the conclusions we can draw. Class-level precision, recall, F1 and confusion-matrix errors provide a more complete picture than accuracy alone.');heading('Future improvements');table([['Priority','Next experiment','Evaluation focus'],['1','Collect more diverse, consistently labeled images','Per-class F1 and support'],['2','Group splits by object and capture session','Reduce near-duplicate leakage'],['3','Clarify or refine the broad trash label','Label consistency and false positives'],['4','Fine-tune upper CNN layers and compare with frozen baseline','Validation-selected model; untouched test set'],['5','Evaluate on independently collected images','Performance under new backgrounds and lighting']],[45,245,205]);p('Conclusion: the CNN meets the stated image-classification objective on the held-out TrashNet split and clearly outperforms the majority baseline. Further data and evaluation are needed before making claims about real-world sorting performance.')
page('Reproducibility and implementation checks')
